update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$The mean uses every value, so a single extreme value drags it — that is its main weakness$cm$::text, $cm$Then E(X) = (a + b)/2 and Var(X) = (b − a)��/12 — memorise both; they are quick exam marks$cm$::text, $cm$sin(A ± B) = sin A cos B ± cos A sin B$cm$::text, $cm$The range is max − min — quick but destroyed by a single outlier$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes: “The range is max − min — quick but destroyed by a single outlier”.$cm$ where id = 'c97cab88-c4fb-4cd2-bc42-5b2f94d9ab97';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Q₁ at position (n + 1)/4, Q₃ at 3(n + 1)/4 (raw data). The interquartile range, IQR = Q₃ − Q₁, is the spread of the middle 50% and resists outliers$cm$::text, $cm$Choosing a committee of 3 from 8 people: ⁸C₃ = 56. Choosing is not arranging — a committee has no internal order, so we divide by r!$cm$::text, $cm$For grouped data the modal class is the one with the highest frequency density (frequency ÷ class width), not the highest frequency$cm$::text, $cm$At least / at most: split into cases and add. A team of 5 from 6 boys and 5 girls with at least 3 girls is ⁵C₃⁶C₂ + ⁵C₄⁶C₁ + ⁵C₅⁶C₀$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes: “Q₁ at position (n + 1)/4, Q₃ at 3(n + 1)/4 (raw data). The interquartile range, IQR = Q₃ − Q₁, is the spread of the middle 50% and resists outliers”.$cm$ where id = '09be9968-577f-4d59-95d9-89129ece112a';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$For x = f(t), y = g(t): dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt)$cm$::text, $cm$Outliers are commonly flagged as values beyond Q₁ − 1.5·IQR or Q₃ + 1.5·IQR$cm$::text, $cm$Any missing probability is found by subtraction$cm$::text, $cm$P(X = r) = ⁿCᵣ pʳ(1 − p)ⁿ⁻ʳ for r = 0, 1,., n. The ⁿCᵣ counts which r of the n trials were the successes$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes: “Outliers are commonly flagged as values beyond Q₁ − 1.5·IQR or Q₃ + 1.5·IQR”.$cm$ where id = '455fb785-7b83-4e12-bce4-27ace5329e4c';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$P(X = r) = e^(−λ) λʳ / r! for r = 0, 1, 2,$cm$::text, $cm$E(x̄) = μ always holds for the sample mean$cm$::text, $cm$variance s² = Σ(x − x̄)²/n (the definitional form)$cm$::text, $cm$Repeated linear: (4x + 1)/(x + 1)² = A/(x + 1) + B/(x + 1)²$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes: “variance s² = Σ(x − x̄)²/n (the definitional form)”.$cm$ where id = '25d822de-4e96-46b9-9c02-5ac6a23f5750';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$If p changes between trials it is not binomial$cm$::text, $cm$For X ~ B(20, 0.4): E(X) = 8 and Var(X) = 4.8$cm$::text, $cm$[r(cos θ + i sin θ)]ⁿ = rⁿ(cos nθ + i sin nθ)$cm$::text, $cm$equivalently s² = Σx²/n − x̄² (the computational form)$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes: “equivalently s² = Σx²/n − x̄² (the computational form)”.$cm$ where id = '2213cc99-ade9-481d-8a78-5d65e6ca5bfc';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$standard deviation s = √(variance)$cm$::text, $cm$E(x̄) = μ always holds for the sample mean$cm$::text, $cm$sin x → cos x; cos x → −sin x; tan x → sec²x$cm$::text, $cm$For X ~ B(20, 0.4): E(X) = 8 and Var(X) = 4.8$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes: “standard deviation s = √(variance)”.$cm$ where id = 'b0a6d597-a23f-4189-b3c2-98b8f6b34cc3';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Two-tailed tests split α across both tails: compare against α/2 on each side, or equivalently check whether the observation falls in the extreme 2.5% $cm$::text, $cm$The standard deviation shares the units of the data; the variance does not. For frequency tables use Σf(x − x̄)²/Σf, or Σfx²/Σf − x̄²$cm$::text, $cm$Never just say "reject H₀". Conclude in the language of the problem: "there is sufficient evidence at the 5% level that the proportion has increased".$cm$::text, $cm$Plot z as the point (a, b). |z| = √(a² + b²) is the modulus (distance from origin); arg z = tan⁻¹(b/a) is the argument (angle). Multiply two numbers: $cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes: “The standard deviation shares the units of the data; the variance does not. For frequency tables use Σf(x − x̄)²/Σf, or Σfx²/Σf − x̄²”.$cm$ where id = 'b2001f47-da30-4bb9-b73e-28502ccdc4a0';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Check all four before using the model — "justifying a binomial model" is a real exam question$cm$::text, $cm$The mean uses every value, so a single extreme value drags it — that is its main weakness$cm$::text, $cm$Use class midpoints as x. The result is an estimate — grouping discards within-class detail$cm$::text, $cm$This underpins every counting problem: a menu with 4 starters and 6 mains gives 24 meals$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes: “Use class midpoints as x. The result is an estimate — grouping discards within-class detail”.$cm$ where id = '38a92fa3-dd4a-45b0-b224-72df65765bef';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Single points have zero probability, so strict or loose inequalities make no difference: P(X < 2) = P(X ≤ 2)$cm$::text, $cm$Discrete random variables: expectation, variance, E(aX+b), and combining independent variables$cm$::text, $cm$Apply a continuity correction: to approximate P(X ≥ 12) for a binomial, compute P(Y > 11.5) for the normal Y$cm$::text, $cm$Two merged datasets: compute combined Σx and Σx² from each part, then apply the computational formula$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes: “Two merged datasets: compute combined Σx and Σx² from each part, then apply the computational formula”.$cm$ where id = '20535c48-3bfb-46f1-a488-5db87c38fda0';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$When comparing consistency, the dataset with the smaller standard deviation (relative to its mean) is the more consistent$cm$::text, $cm$Choosing a committee of 3 from 8 people: ⁸C₃ = 56. Choosing is not arranging — a committee has no internal order, so we divide by r!$cm$::text, $cm$Then read probabilities from the normal table. For X ~ N(50, 4²): P(X > 56) = P(Z > 1.5) = 1 − 0.9332 = 0.0668$cm$::text, $cm$When asked to compare two distributions, pair a central measure with a spread measure; a mean without a spread is only half an answer$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes: “When comparing consistency, the dataset with the smaller standard deviation (relative to its mean) is the more consistent”.$cm$ where id = '3a5cbfeb-7f43-47fb-8638-2648a1985594';
update public.quizzes set description = $cm$Recall practice built from the Measures of Variation and Standard Deviation study notes — check each statement against what you revised.$cm$ where id = '96daad31-cb36-490a-a064-37fad3a24b80';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Poisson Distribution" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Classic settings: calls per hour at a helpdesk, typos per page, cars per minute at a quiet junction$cm$::text, $cm$It is a weighted average — the long-run average value over many repetitions. E(X) need not equal any value X can actually take$cm$::text, $cm$The median is the middle value: position (n + 1)/2 for ordered raw data$cm$::text, $cm$z = r(cos θ + i sin θ), r = |z|, θ = arg z. Useful for powers and roots$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Poisson Distribution study notes: “Classic settings: calls per hour at a helpdesk, typos per page, cars per minute at a quiet junction”.$cm$ where id = 'b6886285-8f07-43f5-abaa-b60c641c96d3';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Poisson Distribution" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$equivalently s² = Σx²/n − x̄² (the computational form)$cm$::text, $cm$P(X = r) = e^(−λ) λʳ / r! for r = 0, 1, 2,$cm$::text, $cm$For an event A with P(A) = p$cm$::text, $cm$E(x̄) = μ and Var(x̄) = σ²/n$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Poisson Distribution study notes: “P(X = r) = e^(−λ) λʳ / r! for r = 0, 1, 2,”.$cm$ where id = '6eea0037-dff6-47ce-8069-816883ae36e6';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Poisson Distribution" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Standard error means √(σ²/n) — smaller with larger n$cm$::text, $cm$H₀: p = 0.3 against H₁: p > 0.3, observed 10 successes in n = 20$cm$::text, $cm$E(X) = λ and Var(X) = λ — the mean and variance are equal$cm$::text, $cm$The range is max − min — quick but destroyed by a single outlier$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Poisson Distribution study notes: “E(X) = λ and Var(X) = λ — the mean and variance are equal”.$cm$ where id = 'f571c6d9-34f6-4a0c-806e-b0ebf8d535b4';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Poisson Distribution" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$H₀: p = 0.3 against H₁: p > 0.3, observed 10 successes in n = 20$cm$::text, $cm$The range is max − min — quick but destroyed by a single outlier$cm$::text, $cm$Distinct linear: (7x + 4)/(x + 1)(x + 2) = A/(x + 1) + B/(x + 2)$cm$::text, $cm$If a question gives a mean m, then λ = m; the variance is also m$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Poisson Distribution study notes: “If a question gives a mean m, then λ = m; the variance is also m”.$cm$ where id = '1079a433-80c4-4302-8633-6466e7b95e86';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Poisson Distribution" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$If X ~ Po(λ) per hour, then 3 hours gives Y ~ Po(3λ). Rates must be adjusted to the same interval before computing anything$cm$::text, $cm$At least / at most: split into cases and add. A team of 5 from 6 boys and 5 girls with at least 3 girls is ⁵C₃⁶C₂ + ⁵C₄⁶C₁ + ⁵C₅⁶C₀$cm$::text, $cm$A vector a = 3i + 4j has magnitude |a| = √(3² + 4²) = 5. Parallel vectors are scalar multiples; unit vector = a/|a|$cm$::text, $cm$Choosing a committee of 3 from 8 people: ⁸C₃ = 56. Choosing is not arranging — a committee has no internal order, so we divide by r!$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Poisson Distribution study notes: “If X ~ Po(λ) per hour, then 3 hours gives Y ~ Po(3λ). Rates must be adjusted to the same interval before computing anything”.$cm$ where id = 'c2aae184-3c11-413c-bde1-3295a67e9c99';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Poisson Distribution" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$X ~ N(μ, σ²). The curve is fully determined by μ (centre) and σ (width). Total probability is 1, and because X is continuous, P(X = a) = 0 for any sin$cm$::text, $cm$If X ~ Po(λ₁) and Y ~ Po(λ₂) are independent, then X + Y ~ Po(λ₁ + λ₂). Two independent queues merging into one server produce a Poisson with summed r$cm$::text, $cm$P(X < 30) = 0.15 with unknown σ. From tables z = −1.036, so (30 − μ)/σ = −1.036 — one equation per unknown; use a second condition if both are missing$cm$::text, $cm$The median m solves ∫₋∞ᵐ f(x) dx = 0.5 (left half of the area). The mode is the x maximising f(x). For uniform densities the median is the midpoint$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Poisson Distribution study notes: “If X ~ Po(λ₁) and Y ~ Po(λ₂) are independent, then X + Y ~ Po(λ₁ + λ₂). Two independent queues merging into one server produce a Poisson with summed r”.$cm$ where id = 'f4035f63-0578-40cc-b875-8a3c83a1fa9b';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Poisson Distribution" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$In games of chance, set the expected profit to zero to find a fair entry fee$cm$::text, $cm$The estimator with divisor (n − 1) is the unbiased sample variance$cm$::text, $cm$In "at least" questions use the complement exactly as with the binomial$cm$::text, $cm$At A2, state which estimator you are using — the unbiased one is the default$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Poisson Distribution study notes: “In "at least" questions use the complement exactly as with the binomial”.$cm$ where id = 'e7f0285a-cd14-4ab3-b1fb-6e0d027be1bb';
