update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Quadratics" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$If not, restrict the domain (e.g. f(x) = x² for x ≥ 0 has f⁻¹(x) = √x). Graphically, y = f⁻¹(x) is the reflection of y = f(x) in the line y = x, and t$cm$::text, $cm$Distance: √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²). The line from (0,0) to (3,4) has length 5 — recognise 3-4-5 triangles$cm$::text, $cm$A quadratic has the form ax² + bx + c with a ≠ 0. Master three tools: completing the square, the discriminant, and inequalities$cm$::text, $cm$A ≈ h/2 [y₀ + 2(y₁ + … + yₙ₋₁) + yₙ], h = (b − a)/n. More strips → better estimate; always an over-estimate for convex curves$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Quadratics study notes: “A quadratic has the form ax² + bx + c with a ≠ 0. Master three tools: completing the square, the discriminant, and inequalities”.$cm$ where id = '7f014952-381a-4d59-99d2-985aa5da6da5';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Quadratics" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$For positive integer n, coefficients come from Pascal's triangle: (1 + 2x)⁴ = 1 + 8x + 24x² + 32x³ + 16x⁴$cm$::text, $cm$Examples: ∫ x³ dx = x⁴/4 + c; ∫ 1/x dx = ln|x| + c; ∫ sin x dx = −cos x + c; ∫ e^x dx = e^x + c$cm$::text, $cm$If the curve dips below the axis, split the integral at the roots and add the absolute values$cm$::text, $cm$For a > 1, factor out a first: 2x² + 12x + 19 = 2(x² + 6x) + 19 = 2(x + 3)² − 18 + 19 = 2(x + 3)² + 1$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Quadratics study notes: “For a > 1, factor out a first: 2x² + 12x + 19 = 2(x² + 6x) + 19 = 2(x + 3)² − 18 + 19 = 2(x + 3)² + 1”.$cm$ where id = '1e92653f-b581-4973-ad98-4f7591d4fc76';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Quadratics" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Vertex form a(x − h)² + k gives the minimum/maximum value k at x = h — this is how you find a maximum area or minimum cost$cm$::text, $cm$Use the symmetry of the graphs. For sin x = 1/2 in 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π: the acute answer is π/6, and the second solution is π − π/6 = 5π/6$cm$::text, $cm$The perpendicular bisector of AB passes through the midpoint of AB with gradient −1/m(AB) — equate distances PA = PB for the locus method$cm$::text, $cm$For positive integer n, coefficients come from Pascal's triangle: (1 + 2x)⁴ = 1 + 8x + 24x² + 32x³ + 16x⁴$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Quadratics study notes: “Vertex form a(x − h)² + k gives the minimum/maximum value k at x = h — this is how you find a maximum area or minimum cost”.$cm$ where id = '15c94dd9-a41e-499b-88a1-2ba11894deb9';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Quadratics" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$y = a sin(bx) + c has amplitude a, period 2π/b, and vertical shift c$cm$::text, $cm$Δ = b² − 4ac decides how the curve meets the x-axis$cm$::text, $cm$∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = [F(x)]ₐᵇ = F(b) − F(a). Constants of integration cancel$cm$::text, $cm$d²y/dx² = 0 → test either side$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Quadratics study notes: “Δ = b² − 4ac decides how the curve meets the x-axis”.$cm$ where id = 'b6138ba1-ead8-4558-9f70-cd0c098e4afb';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Quadratics" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$For cos x = 0.5 in 0° ≤ x ≤ 360°: x = 60° and 360° − 60° = 300°$cm$::text, $cm$Perpendicular lines: m₁m₂ = −1 (gradients are negative reciprocals)$cm$::text, $cm$Δ = 0 repeated root touches (tangent)$cm$::text, $cm$Degrees → radians: multiply by π/180$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Quadratics study notes: “Δ = 0 repeated root touches (tangent)”.$cm$ where id = '98a727d8-c9c6-4c7d-8356-f550a3494de5';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Quadratics" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$If not, restrict the domain (e.g. f(x) = x² for x ≥ 0 has f⁻¹(x) = √x). Graphically, y = f⁻¹(x) is the reflection of y = f(x) in the line y = x, and t$cm$::text, $cm$For positive integer n, coefficients come from Pascal's triangle: (1 + 2x)⁴ = 1 + 8x + 24x² + 32x³ + 16x⁴$cm$::text, $cm$The perpendicular bisector of AB passes through the midpoint of AB with gradient −1/m(AB) — equate distances PA = PB for the locus method$cm$::text, $cm$Solve by sketching. For x² − 5x + 4 < 0, factorise: (x − 1)(x − 4) < 0, and the curve is below the axis between the roots, so 1 < x < 4$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Quadratics study notes: “Solve by sketching. For x² − 5x + 4 < 0, factorise: (x − 1)(x − 4) < 0, and the curve is below the axis between the roots, so 1 < x < 4”.$cm$ where id = '82d3dc44-b7ee-4c39-aa39-e98332298c9a';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Quadratics" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Set the equations equal; the resulting quadratic's discriminant tells you if the line is a secant (Δ > 0), tangent (Δ = 0) or misses (Δ < 0)$cm$::text, $cm$The perpendicular bisector of AB passes through the midpoint of AB with gradient −1/m(AB) — equate distances PA = PB for the locus method$cm$::text, $cm$If not, restrict the domain (e.g. f(x) = x² for x ≥ 0 has f⁻¹(x) = √x). Graphically, y = f⁻¹(x) is the reflection of y = f(x) in the line y = x, and t$cm$::text, $cm$Use the symmetry of the graphs. For sin x = 1/2 in 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π: the acute answer is π/6, and the second solution is π − π/6 = 5π/6$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Quadratics study notes: “Set the equations equal; the resulting quadratic's discriminant tells you if the line is a secant (Δ > 0), tangent (Δ = 0) or misses (Δ < 0)”.$cm$ where id = 'c7229e23-bf69-481f-b1aa-6cf7aa570fba';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these does the "Quadratics" notes NOT cover?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$A quadratic has the form ax² + bx + c with a ≠ 0. Master three tools: completing the square, the discriminant, and inequalities$cm$::text, $cm$Use the symmetry of the graphs. For sin x = 1/2 in 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π: the acute answer is π/6, and the second solution is π − π/6 = 5π/6$cm$::text, $cm$For a > 1, factor out a first: 2x² + 12x + 19 = 2(x² + 6x) + 19 = 2(x + 3)² − 18 + 19 = 2(x + 3)² + 1$cm$::text, $cm$Vertex form a(x − h)² + k gives the minimum/maximum value k at x = h — this is how you find a maximum area or minimum cost$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$The other three are all in the Quadratics study notes; this one is not.$cm$ where id = 'f1bcec21-9b75-4222-88be-e79e28ef4107';
