update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these does the "Nuclear Physics" notes NOT cover?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Protons + neutrons (nucleons). Nuclide notation: ᴬZX — Z protons, A nucleons. Isotopes share Z, differ in N$cm$::text, $cm$½m⟨c²⟩ = (3/2)kT — temperature measures mean translational KE. Also pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩$cm$::text, $cm$Nuclear densities are enormous (~10¹⁷ kg m⁻³) — nearly all mass in a tiny volume$cm$::text, $cm$Half-life: time for half the nuclei to decay. N = N₀ e^(−λt), λ = ln 2 / t½$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$The other three are all in the Nuclear Physics study notes; this one is not.$cm$ where id = '4c28e3b4-9281-4fae-94e7-4847058cc4fc';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is missing from the "Nuclear Physics" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Nuclear densities are enormous (~10¹⁷ kg m⁻³) — nearly all mass in a tiny volume$cm$::text, $cm$Half-life: time for half the nuclei to decay. N = N₀ e^(−λt), λ = ln 2 / t½$cm$::text, $cm$Luminosity L is total power radiated (W). Intensity at distance d: I = L/(4πd²) — the key to distances$cm$::text, $cm$E = mc². Mass difference Δm in nuclear reactions becomes energy; binding energy = energy released assembling the nucleus. 1 u ≈ 931 MeV$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$The other three are all in the Nuclear Physics study notes; this one is not.$cm$ where id = '2ed7d5d6-a89b-4f1a-87d5-6b214468f057';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is NOT part of the "Nuclear Physics" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Half-life: time for half the nuclei to decay. N = N₀ e^(−λt), λ = ln 2 / t½$cm$::text, $cm$E = mc². Mass difference Δm in nuclear reactions becomes energy; binding energy = energy released assembling the nucleus. 1 u ≈ 931 MeV$cm$::text, $cm$Both release energy by moving toward iron (peak binding energy per nucleon): heavy nuclei split (fission, reactors), light nuclei merge (fusion, stars$cm$::text, $cm$Damping removes energy: light damping → slow decay; critical → fastest return without oscillation$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$The other three are all in the Nuclear Physics study notes; this one is not.$cm$ where id = '726a15fd-e404-4a37-82b2-0d0193e6b599';
update public.quizzes set description = $cm$Recall practice built from the Nuclear Physics study notes — check each statement against what you revised.$cm$ where id = '8fa3c3de-6408-4032-a719-214b7d8cc8b0';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Integration" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$For a > 1, factor out a first: 2x² + 12x + 19 = 2(x² + 6x) + 19 = 2(x + 3)² − 18 + 19 = 2(x + 3)² + 1$cm$::text, $cm$Reverse the power rule and add a constant: ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + c (n ≠ −1)$cm$::text, $cm$Δ = b² − 4ac decides how the curve meets the x-axis$cm$::text, $cm$Distance: √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²). The line from (0,0) to (3,4) has length 5 — recognise 3-4-5 triangles$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Integration study notes: “Reverse the power rule and add a constant: ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + c (n ≠ −1)”.$cm$ where id = '5034bc31-2a9f-46c2-8d10-88f7611337c1';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Integration" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Gradient m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁). From ax + by + c = 0, the gradient is −a/b$cm$::text, $cm$Vertex form a(x − h)² + k gives the minimum/maximum value k at x = h — this is how you find a maximum area or minimum cost$cm$::text, $cm$Examples: ∫ x³ dx = x⁴/4 + c; ∫ 1/x dx = ln|x| + c; ∫ sin x dx = −cos x + c; ∫ e^x dx = e^x + c$cm$::text, $cm$y = a sin(bx) + c has amplitude a, period 2π/b, and vertical shift c$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Integration study notes: “Examples: ∫ x³ dx = x⁴/4 + c; ∫ 1/x dx = ln|x| + c; ∫ sin x dx = −cos x + c; ∫ e^x dx = e^x + c”.$cm$ where id = '29c6e5f7-b86f-4a65-9473-2832f659c6c9';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Integration" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Sum to infinity (|r| < 1 only): S∞ = a/(1 − r)$cm$::text, $cm$If sin x = 0.6 with x acute, then cos x = √(1 − 0.36) = 0.8 (positive in the first quadrant)$cm$::text, $cm$y = a sin(bx) + c has amplitude a, period 2π/b, and vertical shift c$cm$::text, $cm$∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = [F(x)]ₐᵇ = F(b) − F(a). Constants of integration cancel$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Integration study notes: “∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = [F(x)]ₐᵇ = F(b) − F(a). Constants of integration cancel”.$cm$ where id = '6fc39799-3c85-4be6-811a-1457efe270d4';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Integration" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Area between a curve and the x-axis = ∫ₐᵇ y dx, where y ≥ 0$cm$::text, $cm$An inverse exists only if f is one-one$cm$::text, $cm$The tangent gradient at a point is dy/dx there; the normal gradient is −1/(dy/dx)$cm$::text, $cm$Δ = 0 repeated root touches (tangent)$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Integration study notes: “Area between a curve and the x-axis = ∫ₐᵇ y dx, where y ≥ 0”.$cm$ where id = '1b7e3ded-edb7-427e-938a-1ac080a77cf9';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Integration" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Perpendicular lines: m₁m₂ = −1 (gradients are negative reciprocals)$cm$::text, $cm$If the curve dips below the axis, split the integral at the roots and add the absolute values$cm$::text, $cm$Vertex form a(x − h)² + k gives the minimum/maximum value k at x = h — this is how you find a maximum area or minimum cost$cm$::text, $cm$For cos x = 0.5 in 0° ≤ x ≤ 360°: x = 60° and 360° − 60° = 300°$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Integration study notes: “If the curve dips below the axis, split the integral at the roots and add the absolute values”.$cm$ where id = '82dda7f9-1268-44e4-8066-cdd8e6b29479';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Integration" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$A quadratic has the form ax² + bx + c with a ≠ 0. Master three tools: completing the square, the discriminant, and inequalities$cm$::text, $cm$Vertex form a(x − h)² + k gives the minimum/maximum value k at x = h — this is how you find a maximum area or minimum cost$cm$::text, $cm$A ≈ h/2 [y₀ + 2(y₁ + … + yₙ₋₁) + yₙ], h = (b − a)/n. More strips → better estimate; always an over-estimate for convex curves$cm$::text, $cm$Use the symmetry of the graphs. For sin x = 1/2 in 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π: the acute answer is π/6, and the second solution is π − π/6 = 5π/6$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Integration study notes: “A ≈ h/2 [y₀ + 2(y₁ + … + yₙ₋₁) + yₙ], h = (b − a)/n. More strips → better estimate; always an over-estimate for convex curves”.$cm$ where id = '81483cb1-5886-498b-8182-af7847d9ddd5';
