update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Binomial Distribution" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$For X ~ B(20, 0.4): E(X) = 8 and Var(X) = 4.8$cm$::text, $cm$Its boundary is the critical value$cm$::text, $cm$standard deviation s = √(variance)$cm$::text, $cm$a · b = |a||b| cos θ = a₁b₁ + a₂b₂$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Binomial Distribution study notes: “For X ~ B(20, 0.4): E(X) = 8 and Var(X) = 4.8”.$cm$ where id = 'd11ae39d-a9ee-4ced-8f63-ba32560248ee';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Binomial Distribution" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$For grouped data the modal class is the one with the highest frequency density (frequency ÷ class width), not the highest frequency$cm$::text, $cm$If trials are dependent (drawing without replacement from a small population) binomial is not appropriate$cm$::text, $cm$At least / at most: split into cases and add. A team of 5 from 6 boys and 5 girls with at least 3 girls is ⁵C₃⁶C₂ + ⁵C₄⁶C₁ + ⁵C₅⁶C₀$cm$::text, $cm$Solving |2x − 1| = 5: split into 2x − 1 = 5 or 2x − 1 = −5, giving x = 3 or −2$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Binomial Distribution study notes: “If trials are dependent (drawing without replacement from a small population) binomial is not appropriate”.$cm$ where id = 'd5445cec-ef93-4b5e-af59-c55f20112473';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Binomial Distribution" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$The number of ways of choosing r objects from n is$cm$::text, $cm$P(X = r) = e^(−λ) λʳ / r! for r = 0, 1, 2,$cm$::text, $cm$If p changes between trials it is not binomial$cm$::text, $cm$E(aX + b) = a·E(X) + b and Var(aX + b) = a²·Var(X)$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Binomial Distribution study notes: “If p changes between trials it is not binomial”.$cm$ where id = 'ee5852d6-5285-4d17-921c-c7ce97d9e1b2';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Binomial Distribution" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Words with repeated letters: divide by the factorials of each repeat count$cm$::text, $cm$P(X > μ) = 0.5. P(X < a) for a above the mean uses the complement. Tables usually give Φ(z) for z ≥ 0 only; use Φ(−z) = 1 − Φ(z)$cm$::text, $cm$For large n with p near ½, X ~ B(n, p) ≈ N(np, np(1 − p)). The condition usually quoted at this level is np > 5 and n(1 − p) > 5$cm$::text, $cm$When n is large and p small, the binomial is approximately Poisson — but at A2 you usually stay exact$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Binomial Distribution study notes: “When n is large and p small, the binomial is approximately Poisson — but at A2 you usually stay exact”.$cm$ where id = '9d852bab-a1b6-46c2-86cf-124bb9983305';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Binomial Distribution" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$For P(X ≥ k) always rewrite as 1 − P(X ≤ k − 1). Tables give cumulative values — use them rather than summing by hand$cm$::text, $cm$P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B) — the probability of A given that B has occurred. The condition shrinks the sample space$cm$::text, $cm$Why n − 1: deviations are measured from x̄ rather than μ, slightly underestimating spread; the smaller divisor corrects it$cm$::text, $cm$The line through point A with direction d: r = a + t d. Two lines are parallel if their directions are multiples$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Binomial Distribution study notes: “For P(X ≥ k) always rewrite as 1 − P(X ≤ k − 1). Tables give cumulative values — use them rather than summing by hand”.$cm$ where id = 'c3a89813-df2f-4bd2-a3c4-3aaab856bb6e';
