update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "The Normal Distribution" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Plot z as the point (a, b). |z| = √(a² + b²) is the modulus (distance from origin); arg z = tan⁻¹(b/a) is the argument (angle). Multiply two numbers: $cm$::text, $cm$If X ~ Po(λ₁) and Y ~ Po(λ₂) are independent, then X + Y ~ Po(λ₁ + λ₂). Two independent queues merging into one server produce a Poisson with summed r$cm$::text, $cm$X ~ N(μ, σ²). The curve is fully determined by μ (centre) and σ (width). Total probability is 1, and because X is continuous, P(X = a) = 0 for any sin$cm$::text, $cm$A and B are independent exactly when P(A ∩ B) = P(A)·P(B). Do not confuse independent (no influence on probabilities) with mutually exclusive (cannot $cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the The Normal Distribution study notes: “X ~ N(μ, σ²). The curve is fully determined by μ (centre) and σ (width). Total probability is 1, and because X is continuous, P(X = a) = 0 for any sin”.$cm$ where id = '3f36ab77-3bf7-41b3-8f94-0a2750759fd8';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "The Normal Distribution" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$P(X ≥ 2) = 1 − P(X ≤ 1). Keep one extra decimal place in intermediate steps to avoid rounding drift$cm$::text, $cm$When comparing consistency, the dataset with the smaller standard deviation (relative to its mean) is the more consistent$cm$::text, $cm$Classic settings: calls per hour at a helpdesk, typos per page, cars per minute at a quiet junction$cm$::text, $cm$Then read probabilities from the normal table. For X ~ N(50, 4²): P(X > 56) = P(Z > 1.5) = 1 − 0.9332 = 0.0668$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the The Normal Distribution study notes: “Then read probabilities from the normal table. For X ~ N(50, 4²): P(X > 56) = P(Z > 1.5) = 1 − 0.9332 = 0.0668”.$cm$ where id = 'fe2b59d3-399c-4eb6-bfb6-d87042006c51';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "The Normal Distribution" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Given a probability, find μ or σ. Convert the target percentile to a z-value from the table, then solve (x − μ)/σ = z$cm$::text, $cm$For P(X ≥ k) always rewrite as 1 − P(X ≤ k − 1). Tables give cumulative values — use them rather than summing by hand$cm$::text, $cm$i² = −1. A complex number z = a + bi has real part a and imaginary part b. Two numbers are equal iff both parts match$cm$::text, $cm$A vector a = 3i + 4j has magnitude |a| = √(3² + 4²) = 5. Parallel vectors are scalar multiples; unit vector = a/|a|$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the The Normal Distribution study notes: “Given a probability, find μ or σ. Convert the target percentile to a z-value from the table, then solve (x − μ)/σ = z”.$cm$ where id = '7f966c40-e5c7-4495-9a24-b0bdc218113e';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "The Normal Distribution" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Piecewise densities carry an unknown k: impose that the total area is 1 (and continuity at joins where required). One equation, one unknown$cm$::text, $cm$P(X < 30) = 0.15 with unknown σ. From tables z = −1.036, so (30 − μ)/σ = −1.036 — one equation per unknown; use a second condition if both are missing$cm$::text, $cm$If the observed value lies in the critical region, evidence supports H₁; otherwise there is insufficient evidence — never "prove H₀ true"$cm$::text, $cm$The standard deviation shares the units of the data; the variance does not. For frequency tables use Σf(x − x̄)²/Σf, or Σfx²/Σf − x̄²$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the The Normal Distribution study notes: “P(X < 30) = 0.15 with unknown σ. From tables z = −1.036, so (30 − μ)/σ = −1.036 — one equation per unknown; use a second condition if both are missing”.$cm$ where id = '84a8ab4c-556d-4d22-9c01-a8ca577c5220';
