update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Sampling and Estimation" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Whatever the population shape, for large n (rule of thumb n ≥ 30) the sample mean is approximately normal: x̄ ~ N(μ, σ²/n). This is why the normal app$cm$::text, $cm$For n people with 2 who must be separated: arrange the other n − 2 (ways), then place 2 people in distinct gaps between them$cm$::text, $cm$If X ~ Po(λ) per hour, then 3 hours gives Y ~ Po(3λ). Rates must be adjusted to the same interval before computing anything$cm$::text, $cm$When comparing consistency, the dataset with the smaller standard deviation (relative to its mean) is the more consistent$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Sampling and Estimation study notes: “Whatever the population shape, for large n (rule of thumb n ≥ 30) the sample mean is approximately normal: x̄ ~ N(μ, σ²/n). This is why the normal app”.$cm$ where id = 'bc3b3ee4-90fa-4d79-95b6-63323c89b96f';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Sampling and Estimation" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$A continuous variable X is described by a density f(x) with f(x) ≥ 0 and ∫f(x)dx = 1 over all x. Probabilities are areas$cm$::text, $cm$Given sample data, estimate μ by x̄ and σ² by the unbiased s². When the population is normal, x̄ is exactly normal for any n — the CLT is only needed $cm$::text, $cm$The null hypothesis H₀ is the default claim (no change, p = p₀). The alternative H₁ is what a suspicious observation suggests (p > p₀, p < p₀, or p ≠ $cm$::text, $cm$Two-tailed tests split α across both tails: compare against α/2 on each side, or equivalently check whether the observation falls in the extreme 2.5% $cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Sampling and Estimation study notes: “Given sample data, estimate μ by x̄ and σ² by the unbiased s². When the population is normal, x̄ is exactly normal for any n — the CLT is only needed ”.$cm$ where id = '800da274-4055-4538-8f6e-515c4c42d2c1';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Sampling and Estimation" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$sin(A ± B) = sin A cos B ± cos A sin B$cm$::text, $cm$cos(A ± B) = cos A cos B ∓ sin A sin B$cm$::text, $cm$Standard error means √(σ²/n) — smaller with larger n$cm$::text, $cm$A dataset may have no mode or several$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Sampling and Estimation study notes: “Standard error means √(σ²/n) — smaller with larger n”.$cm$ where id = '8b886adc-a135-4779-a66e-2931a678e1c4';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Sampling and Estimation" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Classic settings: calls per hour at a helpdesk, typos per page, cars per minute at a quiet junction$cm$::text, $cm$No two specified people adjacent: arrange everyone else, then slot the restricted people into gaps$cm$::text, $cm$Then read probabilities from the normal table. For X ~ N(50, 4²): P(X > 56) = P(Z > 1.5) = 1 − 0.9332 = 0.0668$cm$::text, $cm$Quote probabilities for x̄ with the variance divided by n; forgetting that division is the classic error$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Sampling and Estimation study notes: “Quote probabilities for x̄ with the variance divided by n; forgetting that division is the classic error”.$cm$ where id = '3b8c3d9b-1b1d-42d5-9691-447b0e28e4ed';
