update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Continuous Random Variables" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$P(X < 30) = 0.15 with unknown σ. From tables z = −1.036, so (30 − μ)/σ = −1.036 — one equation per unknown; use a second condition if both are missing$cm$::text, $cm$When comparing consistency, the dataset with the smaller standard deviation (relative to its mean) is the more consistent$cm$::text, $cm$Why n − 1: deviations are measured from x̄ rather than μ, slightly underestimating spread; the smaller divisor corrects it$cm$::text, $cm$A continuous variable X is described by a density f(x) with f(x) ≥ 0 and ∫f(x)dx = 1 over all x. Probabilities are areas$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Continuous Random Variables study notes: “A continuous variable X is described by a density f(x) with f(x) ≥ 0 and ∫f(x)dx = 1 over all x. Probabilities are areas”.$cm$ where id = '4630823a-d442-436d-b432-561a37730b4d';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Continuous Random Variables" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Single points have zero probability, so strict or loose inequalities make no difference: P(X < 2) = P(X ≤ 2)$cm$::text, $cm$A vector a = 3i + 4j has magnitude |a| = √(3² + 4²) = 5. Parallel vectors are scalar multiples; unit vector = a/|a|$cm$::text, $cm$Perpendicular ⇔ a · b = 0. Parallel ⇔ cross-multiplied components are equal (in 2D: a₁b₂ − a₂b₁ = 0)$cm$::text, $cm$P(X ≥ 2) = 1 − P(X ≤ 1). Keep one extra decimal place in intermediate steps to avoid rounding drift$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Continuous Random Variables study notes: “Single points have zero probability, so strict or loose inequalities make no difference: P(X < 2) = P(X ≤ 2)”.$cm$ where id = '4170aedb-3c16-4d41-a23f-81d88c62402e';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Continuous Random Variables" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$When asked to compare two distributions, pair a central measure with a spread measure; a mean without a spread is only half an answer$cm$::text, $cm$Piecewise densities carry an unknown k: impose that the total area is 1 (and continuity at joins where required). One equation, one unknown$cm$::text, $cm$Fill from the intersection outwards: if P(A) = 0.6, P(B) = 0.5 and P(A ∩ B) = 0.25, then only-A = 0.35, only-B = 0.25, neither = 0.15$cm$::text, $cm$E(X + Y) = E(X) + E(Y) and Var(X + Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y). The addition rule for expectation always holds; the variance addition needs independence$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Continuous Random Variables study notes: “Piecewise densities carry an unknown k: impose that the total area is 1 (and continuity at joins where required). One equation, one unknown”.$cm$ where id = '847b9247-2867-4957-bcbe-bdb157914b27';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Continuous Random Variables" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Solving |2x − 1| = 5: split into 2x − 1 = 5 or 2x − 1 = −5, giving x = 3 or −2$cm$::text, $cm$ARRANGE has 7 letters with A appearing twice and R twice, so 7!/(2!·2!) = 1260$cm$::text, $cm$The mean is the balancing point of the area under the density$cm$::text, $cm$Write E(X²) and (E(X))² separately; merging them is the most common exam error$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Continuous Random Variables study notes: “The mean is the balancing point of the area under the density”.$cm$ where id = '13fa689f-4eef-45a9-bf2e-912742018061';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement belongs to the "Continuous Random Variables" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$P(X > μ) = 0.5. P(X < a) for a above the mean uses the complement. Tables usually give Φ(z) for z ≥ 0 only; use Φ(−z) = 1 − Φ(z)$cm$::text, $cm$For large n with p near ½, X ~ B(n, p) ≈ N(np, np(1 − p)). The condition usually quoted at this level is np > 5 and n(1 − p) > 5$cm$::text, $cm$It is a weighted average — the long-run average value over many repetitions. E(X) need not equal any value X can actually take$cm$::text, $cm$The median m solves ∫₋∞ᵐ f(x) dx = 0.5 (left half of the area). The mode is the x maximising f(x). For uniform densities the median is the midpoint$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$From the Continuous Random Variables study notes: “The median m solves ∫₋∞ᵐ f(x) dx = 0.5 (left half of the area). The mode is the x maximising f(x). For uniform densities the median is the midpoint”.$cm$ where id = 'dc1c499c-be8d-45dc-b1e6-7dd5f23572fe';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is from the "Continuous Random Variables" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$If f(x) = 1/(b − a) on [a, b], every point is equally likely$cm$::text, $cm$The range is max − min — quick but destroyed by a single outlier$cm$::text, $cm$Distinct linear: (7x + 4)/(x + 1)(x + 2) = A/(x + 1) + B/(x + 2)$cm$::text, $cm$If a question gives a mean m, then λ = m; the variance is also m$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$From the Continuous Random Variables study notes: “If f(x) = 1/(b − a) on [a, b], every point is equally likely”.$cm$ where id = 'bc70423c-f42b-4499-9861-1acfec59a9c8';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is covered by the "Continuous Random Variables" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Write E(X²) and (E(X))² separately; merging them is the most common exam error$cm$::text, $cm$Then E(X) = (a + b)/2 and Var(X) = (b − a)��/12 — memorise both; they are quick exam marks$cm$::text, $cm$P(X ≤ 2) = P(0) + P(1) + P(2). "At least" questions convert by the complement$cm$::text, $cm$Arithmetic: add/subtract parts; multiply with brackets and replace i² with −1$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 1, explanation = $cm$From the Continuous Random Variables study notes: “Then E(X) = (a + b)/2 and Var(X) = (b − a)��/12 — memorise both; they are quick exam marks”.$cm$ where id = '8ad3258c-cf24-432f-827b-29704a119781';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$From the "Continuous Random Variables" notes, which statement appears?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Reverse of the chain rule. For ∫ 2x e^(x²) dx, let u = x²: du = 2x dx, so the integral is ∫ e^u du = e^(x²) + c$cm$::text, $cm$where E(X²) = Σxᵢ²pᵢ — square the values before multiplying by probabilities. The standard deviation is √Var(X)$cm$::text, $cm$Median questions are integration-to-0.5 questions; set them up before calculating$cm$::text, $cm$0 ≤ p ≤ 1, and P(A′) = 1 − P(A). For mutually exclusive events, probabilities add$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 2, explanation = $cm$From the Continuous Random Variables study notes: “Median questions are integration-to-0.5 questions; set them up before calculating”.$cm$ where id = '995007a2-e40d-4c3d-bc4f-f9adecfd61a5';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which of these is NOT part of the "Continuous Random Variables" notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$A continuous variable X is described by a density f(x) with f(x) ≥ 0 and ∫f(x)dx = 1 over all x. Probabilities are areas$cm$::text, $cm$Single points have zero probability, so strict or loose inequalities make no difference: P(X < 2) = P(X ≤ 2)$cm$::text, $cm$Piecewise densities carry an unknown k: impose that the total area is 1 (and continuity at joins where required). One equation, one unknown$cm$::text, $cm$The standard deviation shares the units of the data; the variance does not. For frequency tables use Σf(x − x̄)²/Σf, or Σfx²/Σf − x̄²$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 3, explanation = $cm$The other three are all in the Continuous Random Variables study notes; this one is not.$cm$ where id = '17684005-ab02-47e1-ae46-d4f9e644f9a3';
update public.questions set question_text = $cm$Which statement is NOT in the "Continuous Random Variables" study notes?$cm$, options = to_jsonb(array[$cm$Fill from the intersection outwards: if P(A) = 0.6, P(B) = 0.5 and P(A ∩ B) = 0.25, then only-A = 0.35, only-B = 0.25, neither = 0.15$cm$::text, $cm$Single points have zero probability, so strict or loose inequalities make no difference: P(X < 2) = P(X ≤ 2)$cm$::text, $cm$Piecewise densities carry an unknown k: impose that the total area is 1 (and continuity at joins where required). One equation, one unknown$cm$::text, $cm$The mean is the balancing point of the area under the density$cm$::text]::text[]), correct_option = 0, explanation = $cm$The other three are all in the Continuous Random Variables study notes; this one is not.$cm$ where id = '4fc80897-3f0f-4072-8e10-aeb348ca39a1';
